- role: assistant
  content: |
    Here’s a concise, step-by-step manual verification checklist for Iteration 2 (Devices & Groups). It covers both API and UI paths and matches current behavior.

    Start services
    - Apply migrations and start stack:
      - docker compose up -d db
      - docker compose up -d backend
      - docker compose exec backend alembic upgrade head
      - docker compose up -d web
    - Access points:
      - App: http://localhost:8080
      - API via Nginx: http://localhost:8080/api/…
    - Default credentials: owner / owner

    Backend API (via curl)
    - Login and save cookie:
      - curl -s -c cookies.txt -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"username":"owner","password":"owner"}' http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
    - Create a device:
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"name":"devA","description":"","unit":"°C","sampling_interval":30,"thresholds":{"warning":70,"critical":90},"modbus_config":{"type":"tcp","host":"127.0.0.1","port":1502}}' http://localhost:8080/api/devices/
    - List devices:
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt http://localhost:8080/api/devices/
    - Get device detail:
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt http://localhost:8080/api/devices/1
    - Update device:
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -X PATCH -d '{"unit":"C"}' http://localhost:8080/api/devices/1
    - Test Modbus connection (TCP reachability stub):
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/devices/1/test-connection
    - Create groups:
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"name":"Grp1","description":""}' http://localhost:8080/api/groups/
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"name":"Grp2","description":""}' http://localhost:8080/api/groups/
    - Assign device to group (single-group enforced):
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/groups/1/devices/1
      - Reassign to another group (moves it): curl -s -b cookies.txt -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/groups/2/devices/1
    - Unassign device:
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt -X DELETE http://localhost:8080/api/groups/2/devices/1
    - Delete device:
      - curl -s -b cookies.txt -X DELETE http://localhost:8080/api/devices/1

    Frontend (UI)
    - Open http://localhost:8080 and login as owner.
    - Devices:
      - Create device “devA” with unit °C, interval 30, thresholds (70/90), TCP host 127.0.0.1, port 1502.
      - See it in the list; test connection (likely shows unreachable unless a service is listening).
      - Update device (change unit to C); delete device; confirm it disappears.
    - Groups:
      - Create “Grp1” and “Grp2”.
      - Assign device “devA” to “Grp1”.
      - Assign same device to “Grp2” (device moves to Grp2 due to single-group enforcement).
      - Unassign device from “Grp2”.
      - Delete groups and confirm they disappear.

    RBAC checks
    - As viewer (create a viewer first from Users page or API), login and open Devices/Groups:
      - Devices/Groups pages load (view-only).
      - Try creating a device/group → backend returns 403; UI shows an error toast.
    - As admin: same as owner for managing devices/groups.

    Edge cases to try
    - Duplicate device name:
      - POST /api/devices/ with existing name → 409 Conflict
    - Not found:
      - GET /api/devices/999999 or DELETE /api/devices/999999 → 404 Not Found
      - POST /api/groups/1/devices/999999 → 404 (device)
      - DELETE /api/groups/999999 → 404 (group)

    Notes
    - thresholds and modbus_config are returned as JSON objects (expanded) even though stored as strings in DB.
    - Single-group enforcement currently reassigns on second assignment rather than erroring (design choice).
    - Historical readings retention on device delete is a no-op right now (readings arrive in Iteration 3); no historical data exists yet to validate retention.

    Optional: run backend checks locally
    - cd backend
    - python -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/python -m pip install '.[dev]'
    - .venv/bin/python -m ruff check .
    - .venv/bin/python -m mypy .
    - PYTHONPATH=. .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q

    If you want me to execute the Docker and curl verification on your machine and paste outputs, I can do that next.
